  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "Suppose that \\(a\\) cows give \\(b\\) gallons of milk in \\(c\\) days. At this rate, how many gallons of milk will \\(d\\) cows give in \\(e\\) days?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{bde}{ac}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{ac}{bde}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{abde}{c}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{bcde}{a}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{abc}{de}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "At the stated constant rate, production scales with both the number of cows and the number of days. The original cow count \\(a\\) and time \\(c\\) are positive, so division by them is valid.\n\nDividing \\(b\\) gallons by \\(a\\) gives the amount one cow produces in \\(c\\) days. Dividing again by \\(c\\) gives \\(\\frac{b}{ac}\\) gallons per cow per day. Therefore \\(d\\) cows in \\(e\\) days produce\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{ac}\\cdot d\\cdot e\n=\\frac{bde}{ac}\\text{ gallons},\n\\]\nchoice A.",
